[1]         These proceedings demonstrate a difficulty in the Rules of the Supreme Court of British Columbia relating to service of process abroad.  Due to the procedural issues relating to Rule 13 of the Supreme Court Rules and the Hague Convention on the Service Abroad of Judicial and Extrajudicial Documents in Civil or Commercial Matters (November 15, 1965), a chronology is necessary.

1.   March 23, 1998 - writ of summons and statement of claim issued;

2.   March 25, 1998 - personal service effected on all three defendants pursuant to Rule 13(12)(a);

3.   May 14 , 1998 - default judgment with damages to be assessed entered as a desk order;

4.   July 3, 1998 - September 16, 1998 - Hague service on the defendants Kabushiki Kaisha Ikoma and Fujimoto Kenichi;

5.   October 1998 - the plaintiff receives legal advice that personal service in Japan that does not comply with the Hague Convention does not permit enforcement of the judgment;

